### When should you choose Lambda?

Lambda shines for event-driven and intermittent work. When an S3 upload, an SQS message, an API Gateway request, or an EventBridge event occurs, Lambda runs your function, scales automatically with the number of events, and you pay only for the milliseconds it runs. When nothing happens, it costs nothing.

```java
// Lambda handler for S3 event: index a newly uploaded statement PDF
package com.bank.lambda;

import java.util.Map;

public class StatementIndexerHandler {
    // Initialized once on cold start, reused on warm invocations
    private static final IndexingService indexer = new IndexingService();

    public String handleRequest(Map<String, Object> s3Event, Object context) {
        String bucket = extractBucket(s3Event);
        String key = extractKey(s3Event);
        indexer.indexStatement(bucket, key);
        return "Indexed: " + key;
    }

    private String extractBucket(Map<String, Object> event) { return "nbi-statements"; }
    private String extractKey(Map<String, Object> event) { return "2026/10/SAV2001.pdf"; }
}

class IndexingService {
    void indexStatement(String bucket, String key) {
        System.out.println("Indexing statement: " + bucket + "/" + key);
    }
}
```

### When should you keep a container?

Keep a container on ECS or EKS when the workload is long-running, steady, latency-sensitive in a way cold starts would harm, needs more than Lambda's limits, or requires full runtime control. If a function runs flat out around the clock, always-on containers are cheaper than per-millisecond Lambda billing.

```java
// Decision matrix for Lambda vs Container
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class ComputeDecision {
    public static String recommend(int durationMinutes, boolean eventDriven, boolean steadyLoad) {
        if (durationMinutes > 15) return "Container (exceeds Lambda 15-min limit)";
        if (steadyLoad)           return "Container (cheaper when always-on)";
        if (eventDriven)          return "Lambda (scales to zero, pay per use)";
        return "Lambda (default for short, intermittent work)";
    }
}
```

### What is a cold start and how do you mitigate it?

When Lambda needs a fresh execution environment -- the first invocation or when scaling out -- it downloads your code, starts the runtime, and runs initialization. This adds latency from tens of milliseconds to a few seconds. For Java, JVM startup makes this worse. Mitigation strategies include initializing expensive clients outside the handler (so warm invocations skip init), using provisioned concurrency to pre-warm environments, using SnapStart for Java, and keeping deployment packages small.

```java
// Initialize expensive clients ONCE outside the handler
package com.bank.lambda;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.dynamodb.DynamoDbClient;
import java.util.Map;

public class FeeCalculatorHandler {
    // Created on cold start, reused on all warm invocations
    private static final DynamoDbClient dynamo = DynamoDbClient.create();

    public String handleRequest(Map<String, Object> event, Object context) {
        // Fast on warm starts: dynamo client already initialized
        return dynamo.describeEndpoints().toString();
    }
}
```

```text
Cold start timeline:
  [Download code] → [Start JVM/runtime] → [Run static init] → [Handle request]
  |___________ cold start latency __________________________|  |__ fast ____|

Warm invocation:
  [Handle request]   ← no init needed, environment reused
  |__ fast ________|
```

### What is the fifteen-minute execution limit?

Lambda caps execution at fifteen minutes (900 seconds). A function that exceeds this is terminated. Long batch jobs must be run as containers or broken into steps using Step Functions, where each step is a short Lambda and the state machine manages the flow.

```java
// Breaking a long job into Step Functions steps
package com.bank.lambda;

public class LongJobSplitter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("NBI nightly reconciliation: ~40 minutes total");
        System.out.println("  Cannot run as a single Lambda (15-min limit)");
        System.out.println("  Solution 1: ECS container task (no time limit)");
        System.out.println("  Solution 2: Step Functions with 3 Lambda steps:");
        System.out.println("    Step 1: Extract transactions (10 min)");
        System.out.println("    Step 2: Match and reconcile (10 min)");
        System.out.println("    Step 3: Generate report (5 min)");
    }
}
```

### How does memory-CPU coupling work?

You configure memory from 128 MB to 10240 MB, and CPU scales proportionally. At 1769 MB you get one full vCPU. You cannot set CPU independently. A CPU-bound function sometimes needs more memory than it uses just to get more CPU, which affects cost.

```java
// Memory-CPU coupling impact
package com.bank.lambda;

public class MemoryCpuCoupling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Memory -> CPU allocation:");
        System.out.println("  128 MB  -> ~0.07 vCPU (slow)");
        System.out.println("  512 MB  -> ~0.29 vCPU");
        System.out.println("  1769 MB -> 1.0 vCPU (full core)");
        System.out.println("  3538 MB -> 2.0 vCPU");
        System.out.println("  10240 MB -> ~6.0 vCPU");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CPU-bound function may need extra memory just for CPU.");
    }
}
```

### Why is Lambda stateless and what does that mean?

Each invocation may run in a different execution environment. You cannot rely on local disk or in-memory state surviving between invocations. Any state must go to an external store like DynamoDB, S3, or ElastiCache. The small `/tmp` space (up to 10 GB) is ephemeral and only sometimes reused on warm environments.

```java
// Stateless Lambda: state goes to DynamoDB
package com.bank.lambda;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.dynamodb.DynamoDbClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.dynamodb.model.*;
import java.util.Map;

public class StatelessProcessingHandler {
    private static final DynamoDbClient dynamo = DynamoDbClient.create();

    public String handleRequest(Map<String, Object> event, Object context) {
        // Store processing state in DynamoDB, not in memory
        dynamo.putItem(PutItemRequest.builder()
            .tableName("nbi-processing-state")
            .item(Map.of(
                "txnId", AttributeValue.fromS(event.get("txnId").toString()),
                "status", AttributeValue.fromS("PROCESSED")))
            .build());
        return "State saved to DynamoDB, not in Lambda memory";
    }
}
```

### What are additional Lambda limits to be aware of?

Beyond the core constraints, Lambda has limits on payload size (6 MB synchronous, 256 KB async), deployment package size (50 MB zipped, 250 MB unzipped, or 10 GB with container images), concurrent executions per account (default 1000, raisable), and `/tmp` storage (10 GB). Very chatty synchronous chains of Lambdas can be both slow and costly.

```java
// Lambda limits summary
package com.bank.lambda;

public class LambdaLimits {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Execution time:    15 minutes max");
        System.out.println("Memory:            128 MB to 10240 MB");
        System.out.println("Sync payload:      6 MB");
        System.out.println("Async payload:     256 KB");
        System.out.println("Package (zip):     50 MB");
        System.out.println("Package (unzipped):250 MB");
        System.out.println("Container image:   10 GB");
        System.out.println("/tmp storage:      10 GB");
        System.out.println("Concurrency:       1000 default (raisable)");
    }
}
```

### What about provisioned concurrency and SnapStart?

Provisioned concurrency pre-warms a specified number of execution environments so they are always ready, eliminating cold starts for latency-sensitive paths like the payment API. SnapStart (for Java) takes a snapshot of the initialized JVM and restores it on invocation, dramatically reducing Java cold start times.

```java
// Provisioned concurrency: pre-warmed environments
package com.bank.lambda;

public class ProvisionedConcurrencyExplainer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without provisioned concurrency:");
        System.out.println("  First request: cold start (2-5 seconds for Java)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With provisioned concurrency = 10:");
        System.out.println("  10 environments are always warm and ready");
        System.out.println("  First 10 concurrent requests: no cold start");
        System.out.println("  11th concurrent request: cold start (scales out)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("SnapStart (Java): JVM snapshot restored on start");
        System.out.println("  Cold start: ~200ms instead of 2-5 seconds");
    }
}
```
